+//! The gluing lemma's table of a game (experiment E15 of the loops study, `lab/work/loops/study-report.md`, Section 15):
+//! for every state that an optimal set of game N can have on a region of columns, a set of cells of board N − 1 that
+//! the lemma proves playable with the rest of the optimal set, which bounds δ(N) = opt(N − 1) + N − opt(N) from below.
+//!
+//! ```text
+//! cargo run --release --example gluing -- family C [--no-sources] [--table FILE]
+//! cargo run --release --example gluing -- game N --base Q,... [--pool Q,...] [--max-columns K] [--need D]
+//!     [--best FILE] [--threads K] [--out FILE] [--stride S] [--limit K] [--node-limit X]
+//! ```
+//!
+//! **The model.**  A *region* is a set of columns, one for each of some primes P with 2P² > N.  Its *cells* are the
+//! numbers k·P ≤ N with k composite, k the cell's *row* or *hub*.  A cell lists its *column factors* (k/q)·P, one for
+//! each prime q dividing k, and its hub k.  The setup checks on the tables of N (`Tables::max_factors` and
+//! `max_composites`) the three facts that 2P² > N gives: a cell's maximal factors are its column factors and its hub; a
+//! column factor is a maximal factor of cells of its own column only; and a cell is a maximal factor of cells of its
+//! own column only.  *Sources* are the cells above N/2, *candidates* the others.  A column factor of a prime row is a
+//! number of layer 2 up to N/2, never a move of an optimal set from 187 on (F4 and F10).
+//!
+//! **States.**  Given statement U at N, some optimal set S of N holds U\*(N), so on the region it holds exactly the
+//! sources in U\*(N), N among them.  Its *state* is the set of candidates it holds.  A state has no core in its own
+//! window instance (members its cells; pool every hub and every column factor that is not a member), since such a core
+//! is a core of S.  The core test is the dismantling of the core theorem (P7), checked against the crate's peel
+//! (`playable::playable_against`) on every base state.
+//!
+//! **The gluing lemma** (study-report.md, Section 15.2).  For a set Z of cells, a nonempty X ⊆ Z is *column-closed in
+//! Z* when every column factor outside Z that a member of X lists is listed by another member of X.  Its *profile* is
+//! the pair (rows X uses at least once, rows X uses at least twice).  Let W be S's cells and O = S − W.  If every X
+//! column-closed in a set W′ of cells of board N − 1 has a Y column-closed in W that uses every row X uses and uses
+//! once only rows that X uses once, then O ∪ W′ is playable on board N − 1, and δ(N) ≥ ΣW′ − ΣW + N.  Say then that W
+//! *covers* W′.  A *witness* of a state is a W′ that it covers with ΣW′ − ΣW + N at least the target D; with the
+//! sources of W′ those of W less N, that is a gain of D on the candidates.
+//!
+//! **How the covering is decided.**  The column-closed sets of a column are found by a search over its cells, and a set
+//! is column-closed exactly when each of its columns' parts is (or is empty).  Profiles combine column by column:
+//! adding a column's part m to a profile (a, b) gives (a | m, b | (a & m)).  Say that p *stands for* x when p uses every
+//! row x uses and uses once only rows x uses once; then Y can be chosen for X exactly when its profile stands for X's,
+//! the relation is a preorder, and it survives adding the same part on both sides.  So only the profiles that nothing
+//! else stands for are kept, on both sides, at every column.
+//!
+//! **What `game` does.**  The base region's states are enumerated.  For each, the search looks for a witness on board
+//! N − 1 over the whole region, column by column, with the covering checked as each column is chosen (any X made of the
+//! columns chosen so far is an X of the final W′), the core test of the partial set, and a bound on the gain; it tries
+//! one changed column, then two, then three, then any.  A state with no witness is *excluded* if a heavier set of cells
+//! of board N (holding the same sources) is covered by it, since O with it would beat the optimal S.  A state neither
+//! closed nor excluded is extended by a column of `--pool`, chosen by the hubs that the uncovered X's use once, and each
+//! extension (the new column's held sources and any of its candidates, without a core) is solved again, up to
+//! `--max-columns` columns; a state still open at the cap, or with no column named, is reported with its hardest
+//! uncovered X.  Every base state's leaves go to `--out` as one JSON line, so a run resumes where it stopped.
+//!
+//! The recorded set of N (the committed one, or the best-known one from `--best`) is solved first: its leaf's witness
+//! W′ is glued to O = S − W, and the crate's peel (`playable::playable`) on board N − 1 decides O ∪ W′, independently of
+//! the lemma, and its score is replayed by the rules.
+//!
+//! **What `family` does.**  The window tables of experiment E12 (`lab/work/loops/e12/window_law.py`) for the families
+//! 8P, 12P and 16P, with the same states, witnesses and output, as a check of the port: n = c·P, columns 0 to d, rows
+//! the composites up to c, weights at the extreme rays of the cone of clean primes.
+
+use std::collections::HashSet;
+use std::fs::{self, File, OpenOptions};
+use std::io::{BufRead, BufReader, Write};
+use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicUsize, Ordering};
+use std::sync::Mutex;
+use std::time::Instant;
+use taxman::board::Board;
